statement:
Let $\mathbb{N}$ denote the set of all natural numbers and let $f : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ be a function such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $f(mn) = f(m) \cdot f(n)$ for all $m, n \in \mathbb{N}$;
\item[(b)] $m + n$ divides $f(m) + f(n)$ for all $m, n \in \mathbb{N}$.
\end{enumerate}
Prove that there exists an odd natural number $k$ such that $f(n) = n^k$ for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.
